WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
Your monitoring dashboard lights up, a critical production-breaking bug has slipped into the live application,

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.920
and active users are experiencing hard crashes. The engineering response is standard.

3
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.120
You trace the stack trace, identify the flawed logic, commit a fix to the main branch,

4
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.360
trigger a fresh release build, and upload the artifact to the play store.

5
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.360
But the process stalls immediately. Google and Apple mandate a platform review for new submissions,

6
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.440
placing your critical fix in a queue that can take days to clear while users continue to face a broken

7
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.440
application. To bypass this exact bottleneck, react native developers utilize over-the-air

8
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:46.960
or OTA updates via EAS. An OTA update pushes a compiled JavaScript bundle directly to the devices

9
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.840
that already have your application installed, operating entirely independent of the app store

10
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.720
infrastructure. By cutting out the platform review, a deployment that previously required a

11
00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.600
three-day waiting period reaches end users in minutes. Because this deliberately method

12
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.080
strictly limits modifications to application logic and user interface components,

13
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.000
it complies fully with both Google Play and Apple App Store policies.

14
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.120
This rapid delivery mechanism is possible entirely because react native's architecture decouples

15
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.080
the static native shell from the dynamic JavaScript bundle. A high-stress deployment emergency

16
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.720
becomes a routine background sync. You pull the release timeline out of the hands of platform

17
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.160
reviewers and return control directly to your engineering team. To deploy these updates effectively,

18
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.800
we have to look past the deployment timeline and examine how a react native application is

19
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.840
physically constructed. The compiled application operates as a two-part structural system,

20
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.760
starting with its external boundary. This model illustrates the outer layer,

21
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.960
a rigid native shell built from Kotlin and Gradle configured by your Android manifest. It houses

22
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.000
device level permissions and native dependencies. Once downloaded from the Play Store,

23
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.920
it is permanently compiled on the user's device. Inside that shell sits the inner layer,

24
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.880
the dynamic JavaScript and TypeScript bundle containing application logic and UI.

25
00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.440
The native shell is fixed, but the JavaScript bundle is interpreted. It can be dynamically swapped

26
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:17.120
without disturbing surrounding curtailed code. This physical reality dictates the foundational

27
00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:23.680
rule of OTA deployments. EAS update only has the mechanical capability to swap this inner

28
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:29.920
JavaScript core. Attempting to modify the native shell over the air will fail. It cannot recompile

29
00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:36.560
Kotlin or execute Gradle on the fly. Visualizing this exact physical boundary allows you to review

30
00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:42.640
a code commit and accurately predict whether the change requires a full-store release. We can translate

31
00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:48.400
this structural model into a diagnostic checklist for routing deployments. This matrix evaluates

32
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.440
specific code changes against our two routing options, a mandatory Play Store release,

33
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.600
or an OTA bypass. Standard modifications to your TypeScript logic or component styling live

34
00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:05.680
entirely within the bundle. They are perfectly eligible for OTA delivery. The same applies to adding

35
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.920
an entirely new application screen, provided it is built exclusively with JavaScript. It fits the inner

36
00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.360
core and routes over the air. But the routing path completely changes the exact moment that your

37
00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:23.920
modifications extend deeply into the native layer. Consider adding a new turbo module written in Kotlin.

38
00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:30.080
This compiled code strikes the rigid shell. A Play Store release is required. Modifying the Android

39
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:36.080
Manifest to request a new camera permission yields the same result. The OTA path is blocked. Adding

40
00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.520
a new native dependency or upgrading the React Native Framework version requires destroying and

41
00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:47.280
replacing the outer shell entirely, mandating a store release. Modifications that touch Gradle,

42
00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:53.200
the Manifest, or require native compilation demand a Play Store release. Code strictly isolated

43
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:59.680
to JavaScript is always OTA eligible. Determining what code is eligible for an OTA update is only the

44
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:05.840
first requirement. You must also route that bundle to specific cohorts of users. An unmanaged deployment

45
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.560
without proper routing could accidentally push a volatile staging bundle directly to your active

46
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:16.320
production users. This topology diagram shows how EAS update manages routing through a decoupled

47
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.000
network divided into three zones, developer workstations, the EAS cloud, and end user devices.

48
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:29.440
Inside the cloud zone are EAS branches, operating as distinct storage repositories for uploaded

49
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:35.600
bundles. Engineers publish a specific state of the code base directly from their workstations to one

50
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:42.720
of these branches. Operating separately from branches are EAS channels. These function purely as delivery

51
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:48.560
pipelines. When compiled, the build profile installed on the user's device is hard coded to

52
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:55.040
subscribe to a specific channel. The routing control comes from this decoupling. Developers dynamically

53
00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:01.120
map a specific branch containing code to a channel pipeline pointing to users. This allows you to

54
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:06.720
thoroughly verify a bundle on a staging branch and then instantly point that exact branch to your

55
00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:13.120
live production channel without triggering a new build. Branches hold the code. Channels deliver it.

56
00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.320
This decoupled topology provides DevOps engineers precise control over release targeting.

57
00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.480
Let's follow the data to the final stage of the process, the exact sequence of events executing on

58
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.440
the client device. When the application is launched or triggered by a background check,

59
00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.680
the device pings its subscribed channel to see if a newer bundle is mapped.

60
00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.040
If a new bundle is detected, the download occurs silently. The user continues navigating the

61
00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.960
current version uninterrupted. The downloaded update is held in local storage. It is only applied over

62
00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.480
the active code upon the following application restart. Zooming out from the individual device,

63
00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:55.200
we can view how this capability integrates into the entire react native release ecosystem.

64
00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.960
This flowchart maps a modern mobile CICD pipeline. You see the heavy track containing

65
00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.960
fast lane, code signing, and EAS built. Parallel to it is the lighter bypass track, EAS update.

66
00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.400
OTA is not a replacement for native pipelines. That heavy track remains strictly required for

67
00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:19.440
processing any structural native layer modifications. But for pure logic changes and rapid hot fixes,

68
00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.680
your deployment routes down the EAS update express track, completely bypassing the native build

69
00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:31.840
and signing stages. Review your deployment pipelines. Verify that your various build profiles

70
00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.720
are accurately mapped to the correct EAS channels for both testing and production.

71
00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.080
By isolating your JavaScript modifications and configuring your channel routing,

72
00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:46.880
you permanently eliminate the multi-day platform review bottleneck for critical bug fixes.

